    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a person or thing. It comes after <em>be, look, feel, sound, taste, smell</em>: <em>She is quick. You look tired. The soup tastes good.</em> An <strong>adverb</strong> says how you do something. It comes after an action verb: <em>She runs <strong>quickly</strong>. He speaks <strong>slowly</strong>.</em>",
        "tag": "Adjective or adverb?"
      },
      {
        "html": "Most adverbs: <strong>adjective + -ly</strong>: <em>quick → quickly, slow → slowly, careful → carefully, quiet → quietly, bad → badly</em>. If the adjective ends in a consonant + <em>-y</em>, change <em>y</em> to <strong>-ily</strong>: <em>easy → easily, happy → happily</em>.",
        "tag": "-ly"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>good</strong> is an adjective. The adverb is <strong>well</strong>: <em>She is a good singer. She sings <strong>well</strong>.</em> Do not say <em>She sings good.</em>",
        "tag": "good → well"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some adverbs look the same as the adjective: <strong>fast, hard, late, early</strong>: <em>He is a fast runner. He runs fast. She works hard. We arrived late.</em> Be careful: <em>hardly</em> means <em>almost not</em>, and <em>lately</em> means <em>recently</em>. <em>She hardly works</em> means she almost never works.",
        "tag": "fast, hard, late"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>She speaks English good.</em> → ✓ <em>She speaks English well.</em> · ✗ <em>He drives very careful.</em> → ✓ <em>He drives very carefully.</em> · ✗ <em>The soup tastes well.</em> → ✓ <em>The soup tastes good.</em> · ✗ <em>They work hardly.</em> → ✓ <em>They work hard.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "An adverb of manner usually comes after the verb, or after the object: <em>She speaks slowly. She speaks English well.</em> Do not put it between the verb and the object. Careful: <em>friendly, lovely, lonely</em> end in <em>-ly</em>, but they are adjectives: <em>She is friendly.</em> For words like <em>always</em> and <em>never</em>, see <a href=\"/adverbs-of-frequency\">Adverbs of frequency</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
